papers, edge-level values fall in the 10-100 range and, after log transform, in a numerically convenient 1-10 range.","first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000231","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The provided section shows literature-derived gene regulatory network construction and mentions an LLM, but the title is truncated and the candidate model/bridge is not clear enough to classify confidently.","evidence_snippet":"PubTator3 table (relation2pubtator3.gz); integrated with a large language model (LLM); gene regulatory relationships from publications"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000231","paper_type":"unclear","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC4_no_foundation_model_evidence","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The provided evidence is too truncated to establish a generative architecture or a foundation-model-like candidate. What is visible is a literature-derived network construction step, not a generative text-bio foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"Gene regulatory relationships were obtained from the pre-annotated PubTator3 table; integrated with a large language model (LLM)"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC4_no_foundation_model_evidence","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"unclear","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000082","candidate_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000082","source_record_id":"rec_000082","source_corpus":"canonical_vlm_include","title":"Unify learns cellular evolution with universal multimodal embeddings","abstract":"Integrating single-cell RNA-sequencing (scRNA-seq) data across species is still technically challenging. Here, the authors report a transfer learning framework designed to integrate scRNA-seq data across species by combining RNA expression with embeddings from protein language models and general-purpose language models. Integrating single-cell RNA-sequencing (scRNA-seq) data across species is hindered by evolutionary divergence, technical batch effects, and the reliance on one-to-one orthologs. Here, we present Unify, a transfer learning methodology that learns universal cell embeddings by defining functionally coherent, multi-modal macrogenes. This is achieved by combining RNA expression with embeddings from protein language models and general-purpose language models. Unify transcends species boundaries, enabling cross-species comparisons beyond strict gene-level homology. Unify corrects batch effects while preserving conserved biological signals across vast evolutionary distances and enables more accurate prediction of perturbation responses across species, such as from mouse to human. Applied to species separated by over 700 million years, Unify reconstructs more accurate multi-species cell-type evolutionary trees and uncovers convergent gene programs. Together, these results establish Unify as a powerful method for comparative single-cell genomics and evolutionary biology.","doi":"","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"selected_full_text_sections":"[data_source, input_representation: Unify learns cellular evolution with universal multimodal embeddings > Abstract]\nIntegrating single-cell RNA-sequencing (scRNA-seq) data across species is hindered by evolutionary divergence, technical batch effects, and the reliance on one-to-one orthologs. Here, we present Unify, a transfer learning methodology that learns universal cell embeddings by defining functionally coherent, multi-modal macrogenes. This is achieved by combining RNA expression with embeddings from protein language models and general-purpose language models. Unify transcends species boundaries, enabling cross-species comparisons beyond strict gene-level homology. Unify corrects batch effects while preserving conserved biological signals across vast evolutionary distances and enables more accurate prediction of perturbation responses across species, such as from mouse to human. Applied to species separated by over 700 million years, Unify reconstructs more accurate multi-species cell-type evolutionary trees and uncovers convergent gene programs. Together, these results establish Unify as a powerful method for comparative single-cell genomics and evolutionary biology.\n\n### Explore related subjects\n\nDiscover the latest articles and news in related subjects.\n\n- [Machine learning](/subjects/machine-learning)\n- [Data integration](/subjects/data-integration)\n- [Evolutionary biology](/subjects/evolutionary-biology)\n- [Gene expression analysis](/subjects/gene-expression-analysis)","first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000082","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Unify combines scRNA-seq with protein- and general-purpose language-model embeddings to build cell embeddings. The language side is auxiliary feature injection for a transfer-learning predictor, not a primary text-bio generative model.","evidence_snippet":"\"combining RNA expression with embeddings from protein language models and general-purpose language models\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000082","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Unify is presented as a transfer learning framework for universal cell embeddings and cross-species prediction, not a generative architecture. The evidence supports broad transferable representations but not generation.","evidence_snippet":"\"learns universal cell embeddings\"; \"transfer learning methodology\"; \"combining RNA expression with embeddings\"; \"prediction of perturbation responses across species\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"application_wrapper","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000106","candidate_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000106","source_record_id":"rec_000106","source_corpus":"canonical_vlm_include","title":"scMIR: a vision-language foundation model for single-cell light microscopy image representation","abstract":"Single-cell light microscopy images have become an important data source for characterizing cell phenotypes, but their complexity and heterogeneity pose challenges to high-throughput automated analysis. Existing representation learning methods mostly rely on task-oriented modeling, which is limited by specific datasets and predefined tasks, making them difficult to generalize across different cell types and microscopy modalities, and experimental conditions. Although general-purpose methods have improved the generalization ability of image representation in recent years, their limited utilization of experimental background and biological context information still poses challenges in complex phenotypic analysis. Here, we propose scMIR, a vision-language foundation model for single-cell light microscopy image representation. By synergistically combining self-supervised image reconstruction with text-guided cross-modal alignment, scMIR can simultaneously encode morphological and biological semantic information in a unified representation space. scMIR is pre-trained on 207,957 image-text pairs, covering various cell types, microscopy modalities, and perturbation conditions. scMIR outperforms existing general models and task-oriented methods as systematically evaluated on various complex tasks using 16 benchmark datasets, including cell classification, clustering, phenotype inference, and batch effect correction tasks. Furthermore, scMIR shows a strong generalization ability across various tasks without requiring task-specific fine-tuning. With its unique advantages, we envision scMIR may promote the standardization and automation of high-throughput phenotyping workflows through supporting various downstream analysis tasks.","doi":"","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"selected_full_text_sections":"[data_source: scMIR: a vision-language foundation model for single-cell light microscopy image representation > MATERIALS AND METHODS > Pre-training datasets]\nWe  curated  six  publicly  available  microscopy  datasets  for  multimodal  pretraining, including  LIVECell,  cpg0000,  PBC,  HPA,  HepG2,  and  B\\_ALL.  Among  them, LIVECell and cpg0000 consist of full-field microscopy images, whereas the remaining datasets provide single-cell images. For LIVECell, we directly used the cell instances provided with the original dataset. For cpg0000, full-field images were segmented into single-cell  instances  using  the  Cellpose  [1]  framework,  with  the  Hoechst  channel serving as the nuclear reference. When segmented cell images did not conform to a square shape, zero-padding was applied to obtain square inputs. For LIVECell, cpg0000, and HPA, the original pretraining and evaluation splits provided by the datasets were preserved, ensuring no overlap between the two subsets. Detailed descriptions of each dataset,  along  with  a  comprehensive  summary  of  the  pretraining  data  statistics,  are provided in Table S1.\n\n[input_representation: scMIR: a vision-language foundation model for single-cell light microscopy image representation > scMIR model]\nscMIR is a pretrained model for learning transferable representations from single-cell microscopy images. During pre-training, scMIR is trained on paired single-cell images and structured textual captions to enable text-guided visual representation learning. In downstream applications, the model takes only single-cell microscopy images as input and  outputs  task-agnostic  image  embeddings.  scMIR  framework  includes  three  key modules:  an  image  encoder,  an  image-text  alignment  module,  and  an  image reconstruction module.","first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000106","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"scMIR is presented as a vision-language foundation model with paired microscopy images and textual captions. The text-guided cross-modal alignment makes the text-bio bridge central to the model.","evidence_snippet":"\"paired single-cell images and structured textual captions\"; \"text-guided cross-modal alignment\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000106","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"scMIR is a pretrained vision-language foundation model with strong transferable representations, but the provided sections only show self-supervised image reconstruction and text-guided alignment. They do not clearly establish a genuinely generative text-bio mechanism.","evidence_snippet":"\"self-supervised image reconstruction with text-guided cross-modal alignment\"; \"pretrained on 207,957 image-text pairs\"; \"task-agnostic image embeddings\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"none","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}},{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000131","candidate_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000131","source_record_id":"rec_000131","source_corpus":"canonical_vlm_include","title":"CellDuality: Unlocking Biological Reasoning in LLMs with Self-Supervised RLVR.","abstract":"Chen Y(1), Tan Z(2), Zhang R(1), Qiu M(1), Chen T(1).","doi":"","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"selected_full_text_sections":"[data_source: CellDuality : Unlocking Biological Reasoning in LLMs with Self-Supervised RLVR > 3. Methodology > 3.3. Training Stage 1: Supervised Fine-Tuning for Capability Cold-Start > 3.3.1. Chain-of-Thought Reasoning Dataset Generation]\nWe construct a comprehensive SFT dataset, 𝒟 SFT , by leveraging powerful teacher models (e.g., GPT-4o, Gemini 2.5 Pro) to generate Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning traces. A critical aspect of our approach is that 𝒟 SFT must equip our model with capabilities for both the primal (forward) reasoning and the complementary dual (inverse) reasoning required in our RL stage. Therefore, we generate and curate distinct data subsets for each direction.\n\n##### Primal Task SFT Data.\n\nFor each of our four core tasks, we generate primal task data. Given an input prompt x i , we prompt a teacher model π teacher to generate N candidate responses { y i , k = ( z i , k , a i , k ) } k = 1 N . We then apply task-specific filtering to select high-quality instances for our primal SFT set, 𝒟 SFT primal .\n\n- **For Classification Tasks (Annotation &amp; Sensitivity)** : We use a strict *Rejection Sampling* protocol. A candidate y i , k is accepted if its final answer a i , k exactly matches the ground-truth label a i ∗ . We define an indicator for correctness as ϵ i , k = I ( a i , k = a i ∗ ) . The accepted set for prompt x i is { y i , k ∣ ϵ i , k = 1 } . This ensures all training examples are factually correct.\n- **For Generative Tasks (Cell &amp; Response Generation)** : As no single, unique ground-truth sequence exists for these tasks, a simple exact match is infeasible. Instead, we adopt a *Rank-Aware Filtering* protocol. For each prompt x i with a corresponding ground-truth cell sequence a i ∗ , the teacher model generates a candidate response ( z i , a i ). The candidate is accepted into 𝒟 SFT only if the generated cell sequence a i demonstrates high fidelity to the ground truth in terms of both gene overlap and expression ranking. We quantify this using our proposed Rank-Weighted Jaccard Similarity metric ( [Eq. 3](#FD5) ). A candidate is accepted only if its similarity score exceeds a predefined threshold.\n\n##### Dual Task SFT Data.\n\nTo explicitly teach the model the inverse reasoning required for our duality framework, we construct a corresponding dual task SFT set, 𝒟 SFT dual . For each instance in our curated primal set, we formulate its complementary dual problem.\n\n- For a primal instance ( x = ( x k , x u ) , y ∗ ), we construct a dual prompt x dual = ( y ∗ , x k ) . The ground-truth answer for this dual task is the original unknown component, y dual ∗ = x u .\n- For example, for a perturbation response instance where x k = c pre , x u = p , and y ∗ = c post ∗ , the dual SFT sample would be: prompt ( c post ∗ , c pre ) paired with the ground-truth answer p .\n\nThe teacher model is then prompted to generate CoT reasoning for these dual problems. The final SFT dataset is the union 𝒟 SFT = 𝒟 SFT primal ∩ 𝒟 SFT dual . This hybrid strategy ensures the model is proficient in both forward and inverse reasoning before entering the RL stage.\n\n[input_representation: CellDuality : Unlocking Biological Reasoning in LLMs with Self-Supervised RLVR > 3. Methodology > 3.1. Preliminaries and Task Formulation]\nWe denote the LLM policy as π θ , parameterized by θ . Let 𝒱 be the global vocabulary of all considered gene names. Our work addresses a structured set of four core single-cell reasoning tasks, organized into a 2x2 matrix spanning two fundamental biological themes: *Cell Identity* and *Cell Dynamics* . The core data structures for these tasks are defined as follows:\n\n- **Cell Representation** : A cell c = { g 1 , g 2 , ... , g K } is represented as an descending order sequence of its top K expressed genes, where each gene g i ∈ 𝒱 .\n- **Perturbation** : A perturbation p is a structured tuple describing an intervention, e.g., p = { operation , target } , where operation ∈ {knockdown, overexpression} and target ∈ 𝒱 .\n- **Cell Type and Sensitivity Labels** : A cell type t , is a categorical label from a predefined set 𝒯 . Similarly, a drug sensitivity label, s , is a categorical label from a set 𝒮 .\n\nAll inputs to the LLM are constructed as textual prompts x that combine these components. The model's output is a textual response y , generated autoregressively according to the policy y ∼ π θ ( ⋅ ∣ x ) . A response may include a reasoning trace z and a final answer a , i.e., y = { z , a } .","first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000131","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The method frames biological reasoning as LLM prompts and textual outputs over cell and perturbation representations. That makes the text component and text-bio bridge explicit in the candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"\"all inputs to the LLM are constructed as textual prompts\"; \"The model's output is a textual response\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"update_2026-08-09__rec_000131","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"CellDuality fine-tunes and steers an existing LLM for biological reasoning with SFT and self-supervised RLVR, so the contribution is an application wrapper rather than a new foundational model. The record does show autoregressive text generation, but not candidate-model foundation pretraining.","evidence_snippet":"\"output is a textual response y, generated autoregressively according to the policy\"; \"leveraging powerful teacher models\"; \"Supervised Fine-Tuning\"; \"RLVR\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"application_wrapper","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}}]

